# Git LFS for the library's PDFs.
#
# TEMPLATE — copied into a library repo as `.gitattributes` by the
# assemble-library skill.
#
# A library is thousands of PDFs and tens of gigabytes. Committing those to git
# directly makes every clone download every version of every file ever, and the
# repository stops being usable long before the library stops growing. LFS keeps
# pointers in git and the bytes in LFS storage.
#
# Set this up BEFORE the first PDF is committed. Converting afterwards means
# rewriting history, and anyone who already cloned has to re-clone.
#
#     git lfs install
#     git add .gitattributes && git commit -m "track PDFs in LFS"
#     # only then start adding PDFs
#
# Check it is working: `git lfs ls-files | head` should list your PDFs. If
# `git cat-file -p HEAD:library/A/Abc1999.pdf | head -c 20` prints `%PDF`
# rather than `version https://git-lfs...`, the file went in as a blob and
# LFS is not actually tracking it.
*.pdf  filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
*.PDF  filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text

# The taxonomy snapshot is small but binary; keeping it out of diffs is kinder.
taxonomy.dwca.zip  binary
